Volume first;
Norepinephrine
as bridge — Definitive treatment is volume resuscitation and haemorrhage/fluid-loss control; norepinephrine is used only as a temporising bridge to support perfusion pressure while that definitive treatment is underway, not as primary therapy. — Vasopressors raise perfusion pressure without correcting the underlying volume deficit, so in hypovolaemic/haemorrhagic shock they are used the same way general critical-care reviews of vasopressor and inotrope therapy frame adjunctive pressor use elsewhere in this algorithm: as bridging support while the causal lesion is actively being fixed, here by blood products, crystalloid, or haemostasis.

Treat cause;
Norepinephrine
as bridge — Definitive treatment is removing the mechanical obstruction (e.g. reperfusion therapy for massive PE, pericardiocentesis for tamponade, needle/chest decompression for tension pneumothorax); norepinephrine again serves only as a bridge while that is arranged. — Because no vasopressor can substitute for relieving a mechanical obstruction to flow, the pharmacological support here is framed identically to the hypovolaemic pathway — a temporising bridge, not a treatment in its own right — consistent with how adjunctive vasopressor use is generally positioned in critically ill patients.

Either drug as second-line therapy
[GRADE: moderate] — Equivalent symptom control. Ondansetron better tolerated (less drowsiness);
metoclopramide if cost or first-trimester exposure concerns predominate. — Either drug is a reasonable second-line choice: symptom control is equivalent, but ondansetron is better tolerated, while metoclopramide may be preferred where cost or first-trimester fetal-exposure concerns predominate. — The largest RCT (Abas, n = 160) found no difference in 24-hour well-being score, vomiting episodes, or nausea trajectory, but drowsiness (30% vs 12.5%) and dry mouth (23.8% vs 10.0%) were roughly twice as frequent with metoclopramide — the drowsiness difference reaches the MCID (d = 0.61) [41], and a Cochrane review confirms both differences (drowsiness risk ratio 2.40; dry mouth risk ratio 2.38) with moderate-quality evidence [52]. A second RCT found a vomiting-score trend favouring ondansetron [42], while the EMPOWER pilot factorial RCT closed without a between-group analysis because most eligible women had already received one of the study drugs [43]. A meta-analysis of 5 RCTs (695 women, 3 of which were not indexed in PubMed and could not be individually appraised) found no important difference in symptom scores or hospital stay [51]. On fetal safety: Danish registry data found no increase in major malformations with either drug [100,101], but a large US cohort (1.8 million pregnancies) found a small increase in oral clefts with first-trimester ondansetron exposure — about three additional cases per 10 000 births [102] — while a Canadian cohort found a modest increase in major malformations with first-trimester metoclopramide, though based on only 31 ondansetron-exposed pregnancies [104]. GRADE certainty is Moderate for both symptom control and the drowsiness/dry-mouth difference. — Fetal/neurodevelopmental safety data point in different directions depending on the cohort: the oral-cleft signal for ondansetron [102] and the malformation signal for metoclopramide [104] both reached statistical significance in their respective cohorts, but a prospective comparative study found no significant difference in major anomalies between the drugs [103], and a Korean birth-cohort study of 630 904 children found no substantial overall neurodevelopmental association for either drug, with only a slightly higher ADHD risk after second-half-of-pregnancy metoclopramide exposure [105] — this is not a settled question. — One source in this evidence base (Klauser, a cohort of home subcutaneous-infusion therapy, ROBINS-I: Serious) contains an internal inconsistency the review itself flags: its own Section 3.5 text states time to symptom improvement was similar (median 2 vs 3 days), while its Table 2 states the reverse (median 3 vs 2 days, OND vs MTC) [44]. — Grading of Recommendations Assessment, Development and Evaluation — A structured framework for rating how confident clinicians should be in a body of evidence, from very low to high certainty, based on risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision and publication bias. — Minimal Clinically Important Difference — The smallest difference in an outcome that patients or clinicians would consider meaningful. This review uses a Cohen's d of 0.5 (half a standard deviation) as the cutoff, so a statistically significant finding below that threshold is not treated as clinically important. — Risk Of Bias In Non-randomised Studies - of Interventions — The companion tool to RoB 2 for assessing bias in non-randomised comparative studies; its ratings top out at ‘serious’ or worse because such studies cannot fully control for confounding by indication.
